Theory 
What is NoSQL Database? 
NoSQL databases are non-relational databases used to store unstructured or 
semi-structured data. They do not use tables like traditional SQL databases. 
What is MongoDB? 
MongoDB is a NoSQL database that stores data in document format 
(JSON/BSON). It is flexible, scalable, and widely used in modern applications. 
Features of MongoDB: 
● Schema-less database  
● High performance  
● Scalable  
● Document-oriented storage  
● Supports indexing and aggregation  
What is CRUD Operation? 
The full form of CRUD is Create, Read, Update, and Delete. These four basic 
functions represent the essential types of operations that can be performed on data 
stored within a database. 
Create Operation 
The "Create" operation refers to the ability to add new records or data entries into a 
database. 
Read Operation 
The "Read" operation encompasses the retrieval of data from a database. It 
involves querying the database to select data based on specific criteria or 
displaying all data within a database. 
Update Operation 
The "Update" function pertains to modifying existing data within the database. 
This operation is vital for maintaining the accuracy and relevance of the stored 
data. 
Delete Operation 
The "Delete" operation refers to the removal of data from a database. It is a critical 
function for managing the lifecycle of data, allowing for the elimination of 
outdated, irrelevant, or unnecessary information.  
What is Aggregation? 
Aggregation is a concept wherein the connection between two entities is 
considered as one unified entity. It includes combining the relationships with their 
respective entities into a better-degree entity. 
Aggregation in MongoDB is used to process and analyze data by performing 
operations like: 
● SUM: It is used for adding things up. 
● AVG: It is used for finding the middle ground. 
● Minimum (MIN): It is used to get the singling out of the smallest values. 
● Maximum (MAX): It is used for singling out for the largest values. 
● COUNT: It is used for a headcount and DISTINCT for counting unique 
items. 
●   $group is used to group data  
●   $sum calculates total  
●   $avg calculates average 
What is Indexing? 
Indexing in databases is a data structure technique used to speed up data retrieval 
operations by minimizing the number of disk accesses required to locate records. 
● Stores copies of selected column (search key) values 
● Maintains pointers to the actual data rows in the main table 
● Allows faster searching without scanning the entire table 
Indexing improves the speed of data retrieval by creating a reference to data. 
Attributes of Indexing 
Several important attributes of indexing affect the performance and efficiency of 
database operations: 
1. Access Types: This refers to the type of access, such as value-based search, 
range access, etc. 
2. Access Time: Refers to the time needed to find a particular data element or 
set of elements. 
3. Insertion Time: To the time taken to find the appropriate space and insert 
new data. 
4. Deletion Time: Time taken to find an item and delete it as well as update the 
index structure. 
5. Space Overhead: It refers to the additional space required by the index. 
